    {
      "type": "context",
      "id": "VZnBK",
      "x": 8172,
      "y": 5684,
      "name": "Conversations nav — mobile",
      "width": 340,
      "height": 0,
      "content": "Conversations nav — mobile\n\nConfirmed Oct 9: the nav opens from the right (chat-nav-refresh's Menu FAB pattern, 240 wide, over a scrim), the press-and-hold hint retires itself, and Safari's bottom bar stays its own thing.\n\nTouch has no hover, so:\n• The More button shows on every conversation row. Rows are 44 tall; More is Hemlock Button/md/text/icon-only.\n• Tap More: Rename · Remove, plus one static line, \"Press and hold a conversation to move it\". The line stops showing once the person has moved a conversation.\n• Press and hold a row (about 500 ms): light haptic tap, the rest of the nav dims, the row lifts in place, and the same menu attaches below it without the hint. Let go without moving to keep the menu; tap outside to dismiss.\n• Move the finger instead: the menu fades and the row follows the finger on the vertical axis, covering the rows it passes. Its old slot stays open until it crosses the next row's middle; then that row slides into it. The list auto-scrolls near its edges. Release to drop, with a second haptic tap.\n\nDrag is the interaction for everyone. Fallbacks stay invisible to people who drag: after press and hold, a tap on another spot in the list moves the row there (WCAG 2.5.7), and screen readers get visually hidden Move up / Move down buttons on each row. No screen-reader detection needed.\n\nEverything binds Hemlock tokens (spacing, text size with its matching line height, radius, shadow) in the mobile typography mode. Safari's chrome keeps iOS's own sizes. Finger circles and the haptic ring are annotations, not UI."
    },
